  789: 
  790:       // (2) AND THE DATA API REFUSES AT THE PRIVILEGE LAYER, on every table that holds tenant data.
  791:       // This is where BOTH shapes are expressible, so both are driven and compared: the migrations
  792:       // grant `anon` nothing at all, so a caller with no token is refused before any policy is
  793:       // consulted, and `rows: null` rather than an empty array is what says so.
  794:       //
  795:       // THE TWO HALVES OF THIS ARM STAND ON DIFFERENT GROUND, and the split is stated rather than
  796:       // left to be discovered. THE NEVER-SIGNED-IN HALF IS SOUND AT BOTH TIERS: that caller sends no
  797:       // `Authorization` header, so PostgREST can resolve it to nothing but `anon`, and `anon` holds
  798:       // no grant. THE EQUALITY WITH THE SIGNED-OUT HALF IS THE FIXTURE'S MODEL. `_live.ts`'s
  799:       // `signOut` deliberately retains the cached tokens, so the live twin of the second call would
  800:       // carry a user's bearer token, and PostgREST judges a token by signature and expiry rather than
