  79: -- WHY A DEFINER FUNCTION AND NOT AN INLINE SUBQUERY, which is the same reason the helper above gives:
  80: -- `public.accounts` carries row-level security with NO policy, so a policy expression that read that
  81: -- table as the querying role would see nothing and every volunteer would be refused.
  82: --
  83: -- IT TAKES NO ARGUMENT, for the reason the helper above states, and it leaks the same nothing: a
  84: -- caller learns only whether IT is a volunteer, which it already knows.
  85: create function public.viewer_is_volunteer()
  86: returns boolean
  87: language sql
  88: stable
  89: security definer
  90: set search_path = ''
  91: as $$
  92:   select exists (
  93:     select 1
  94:       from public.accounts a
  95:      where a.id = (select auth.uid())
  96:        and a.account_type = 'volunteer'::public.account_type
  97:   );
  98: $$;
  99: 
 100: comment on function public.viewer_is_volunteer() is
 101:   'True when the calling user holds a volunteer account row (REQ-001, AT-001.23). Answers only about auth.uid().';
 102: 
 103: revoke execute on function public.viewer_is_volunteer() from public;
 104: grant execute on function public.viewer_is_volunteer() to authenticated, service_role;
 105: 
 106: /* ================================================ the assigned developer's branch ============== */
 107: 
 108: -- AT-001.23's grant, and it is the exact counterpart of AT-001.22's denial: a project's working data
 109: -- is readable by THAT project's assigned developer. `assigned_volunteer_id` is one nullable column
 110: -- holding at most one account, so this clause admits one person per project and there is no
 111: -- collaborator seat for a second to occupy.
 112: --
 113: -- A FREE SEAT ADMITS NOBODY. `assigned_volunteer_id` is null while the seat is free, and `null =
 114: -- auth.uid()` is null rather than true, so an unassigned project stays unreadable through this
 115: -- branch. That is the fail-closed direction and it is a property of the comparison rather than a
 116: -- guard somebody added.
 117: --
 118: -- WHY THE POLICY STATES THE ACCOUNT TYPE ITSELF, RATHER THAN INHERITING IT â€” and this clause is here
 119: -- because of a measurement, not because of a preference. `assigned_volunteer_id` is declared in
 120: -- `20260811130000_single_seat_org_and_single_developer_projects.sql` as a bare
 121: -- `uuid references public.accounts (id)`, and NO constraint and NO trigger restricts the seat
 122: -- holder's account type; the only trigger on that column, `public.project_seat_holds_one_developer()`,
 123: -- enforces the SINGLE-seat invariant and says nothing about the type. The membership seat HAS such a
 124: -- guard â€” `public.org_membership_grantee_must_be_ngo()`, from
 125: -- `20260811125000_org_membership_ngo_only_and_organization_rename.sql` â€” so the two seats are NOT
 126: -- symmetric. Without the second conjunct, any account type placed in a developer seat would read that
 127: -- project's row here.
 128: --
 129: -- AND THAT WOULD MAKE THE DATA API MORE PERMISSIVE THAN THE EDGE SURFACE, which decision C forbids.
 130: -- `visibility.ts`'s project branch requires `accountType === 'volunteer'` before it grants the project
 131: -- scope, so `tenantReadAllowed` refuses the same caller this policy would have admitted. Decision C's
 132: -- whole posture is that both layers enforce, so both must agree; a policy may not inherit an invariant
 133: -- the schema does not enforce.
 134: create policy projects_select_assigned_volunteer
 135:   on public.projects
 136:   for select
 137:   to authenticated
 138:   using (assigned_volunteer_id = (select auth.uid()) and public.viewer_is_volunteer());
 139: 
 140: comment on policy projects_select_assigned_volunteer on public.projects is
 141:   'A project is readable by the volunteer assigned to it (REQ-001, AT-001.23).';
 142: 
 143: /* ================================================ the administrator's reach ==================== */
 144: 
 145: -- AT-001.40 AND THE FOUNDER'S d65 RULING: the platform administrator's role spans all accounts, so
 146: -- one policy per tenant table admits every row of it. FOUR POLICIES RATHER THAN ONE WIDER CLAUSE
 147: -- ADDED TO THE EXISTING ONES: a separate policy leaves each existing tenant rule readable exactly as
 148: -- it was written, and the conformance arm can then say which clause admits a reader by name.
 149: --
 150: -- THE REACH IS NOT NARROWED PER SCOPE, deliberately. `visibility.ts`'s administrator branch does not
 151: -- read the scope either, and narrowing it here would build a rule the founder ruled against.
 152: 
 153: create policy organizations_select_platform_admin
 154:   on public.organizations
 155:   for select
 156:   to authenticated
 157:   using (public.viewer_is_platform_admin());
 158: 
 159: comment on policy organizations_select_platform_admin on public.organizations is
 160:   'Every organisation is readable by a platform administrator (REQ-001, AT-001.40, founder d65).';
 161: 
 162: create policy org_memberships_select_platform_admin
 163:   on public.org_memberships
 164:   for select
 165:   to authenticated
 166:   using (public.viewer_is_platform_admin());
 167: 
 168: comment on policy org_memberships_select_platform_admin on public.org_memberships is
 169:   'Every seat is readable by a platform administrator (REQ-001, AT-001.40, founder d65).';
 170: 
 171: create policy projects_select_platform_admin
 172:   on public.projects
 173:   for select
 174:   to authenticated
 175:   using (public.viewer_is_platform_admin());
 176: 
 177: comment on policy projects_select_platform_admin on public.projects is
 178:   'Every project is readable by a platform administrator (REQ-001, AT-001.40, founder d65).';
 179: 
 180: create policy acknowledgments_select_platform_admin
 181:   on public.acknowledgments
 182:   for select
 183:   to authenticated
 184:   using (public.viewer_is_platform_admin());
 185: 
 186: comment on policy acknowledgments_select_platform_admin on public.acknowledgments is
 187:   'Every acknowledgment is readable by a platform administrator (REQ-001, AT-001.40, founder d65).';
 188: 
 189: -- `public.accounts` AND `public.volunteer_profiles` STILL GET NO POLICY, and the absence is the same
 190: -- deliberate one the previous migration records. Neither table holds data belonging to one tenant in
 191: -- the sense the criteria use, and no criterion in this leaf asks a client key to read either. The
 192: -- conformance arm classifies both as unreachable by the client roles and states which of the two
 193: -- reasons applies to each.
 194: 
 195: -- POSTGREST CACHES THE SCHEMA, AND BOTH HALVES OF THE REASON MATTER HERE.
 196: --
 197: -- THE FUNCTIONS ARE THE HALF THAT NEEDS THIS. `public.viewer_is_platform_admin()` and
 198: -- `public.viewer_is_volunteer()` join the schema PostgREST exposes, so its cache must be told both
 199: -- functions exist; without the reload the cache describes a schema this migration has already changed.
 200: --
 201: -- THE POLICIES ARE THE HALF THAT DOES NOT. A policy is not in that cache at all â€” PostgreSQL applies
 202: -- it inside the query planner on every request, as the querying role, so the four policies above take
 203: -- effect on the next statement whatever any cache holds. The notify is here for the function and is
 204: -- stated as being for the function, rather than left to read as though a policy needed it.
 205: notify pgrst, 'reload schema';
--- supabase/functions/_shared/visibility.ts ---
   1: /**
   2:  * THE TENANT-READ DECISION â€” the one rule that says whether this caller may READ this thing, and the
   3:  * one refusal every non-public read surface answers with.
   4:  *
   5:  * It is a separate module from `./memberships.ts` because the two answer different questions.
   6:  * `memberships.ts` judges whether an ADMIN-ONLY ACTION may be performed in an organisation â€” a write
   7:  * question, on one axis, with two refusal kinds a caller branches on. This module judges READS across
   8:  * three viewer kinds and two scopes, and its refusal is deliberately a SINGLE constant that says
   9:  * nothing at all.
  10:  *
  11:  * IT IS UNDER THE SAME TWO CONSTRAINTS `./memberships.ts` states, for the same two reasons:
  12:  *   1. ZERO NON-RELATIVE IMPORTS AND NO DENO GLOBAL. It is compiled by `tests/at/tsconfig.json`,
  13:  *      which is `strict` with `skipLibCheck: false` and no DOM library, and it is also run by Deno
  14:  *      inside the edge runtime. The intersection is plain TypeScript over plain data.
  15:  *   2. NO I/O, NO CLOCK, NO RANDOMNESS. Every row a decision needs is READ by the caller and handed
  16:  *      here; this module never asks a database anything.
  17:  *
  18:  * ============================================================================================
  19:  * "NO EXISTENCE ORACLE" IS STRUCTURAL HERE, NOT A RULE SOMEBODY APPLIES
  20:  * ============================================================================================
  21:  *
  22:  * AT-001.21's clause is that a denial must not reveal whether the thing exists. That is a property of
  23:  * two answers being IDENTICAL, so the answer itself is exported: `TENANT_NOT_FOUND`. Every non-public
  24:  * read surface returns exactly that constant for BOTH "no such row" and "exists, and is not yours".
  25:  * There is nowhere in the surface to put a second refusal, so the two cannot drift apart through an
  26:  * edit that looks harmless.
  27:  *
  28:  * AND THE SURFACES READ THE TARGET ROW LAST. That ordering is the second half of the property and it
  29:  * lives in the surfaces rather than here, because it is about I/O and this module performs none. It
  30:  * is written down here anyway, because a reader of this constant has to know what makes it enough:
  31:  * every read a decision needs is issued BEFORE the target row is read, and the target read is the
  32:  * LAST read the handler makes. Then a fault answers the same 502 for a real foreign identifier and
  33:  * for one that names nothing, by construction rather than by care. With a lookup AFTER the target
  34:  * read, a fault reachable only on the existing-row path would answer 502 for the foreign identifier
  35:  * while the nonexistent one had already answered 404 â€” an existence oracle outside this constant.
  36:  *
  37:  * WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: TIMING. Response time is a side channel this leaf does not measure and does
  38:  * not defend.
  39:  */
  40: 
  41: import { ACCOUNT_TYPES, type AccountType } from './accounts.ts';
  42: import { parseOrgRole, type OrgRole } from './memberships.ts';
  43: 
  44: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- the one refusal */
  45: 
  46: /**
  47:  * THE ONE ANSWER A NON-PUBLIC READ SURFACE GIVES WHEN IT DOES NOT ANSWER â€” for a row that does not
  48:  * exist AND for a row that exists and belongs to somebody else, with no way to tell which.
  49:  *
  50:  * 404 RATHER THAN 403, AND THE ALTERNATIVE WAS CONSIDERED. `update-organization` answers one 403 for
  51:  * both of its cases and is equally non-oracular; either works so long as it is the SAME answer. 404
  52:  * is chosen because it carries no information at all, while a 403 states that something is there to
  53:  * be forbidden. The cost is that a 404 for a row that does exist is, strictly, not true â€” and that
  54:  * cost is the point of the criterion.
  55:  *
  56:  * IT IS RETURNED, NEVER THROWN. `edgeHandler` in `./edge.ts` turns anything thrown into a shaped 502,
  57:  * so a thrown constant would become a different answer on one of the two paths and the two would
  58:  * diverge through the error path.
  59:  */
  60: export const TENANT_NOT_FOUND = {
  61:   status: 404,
  62:   body: {
  63:     ok: false,
  64:     reason:
  65:       'no such thing is visible to this caller â€” it does not exist, or it exists and is not yours, ' +
  66:       'and this answer deliberately does not say which',
  67:   },
  68: } as const;
  69: 
  70: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------- who is asking, and for what */
  71: 
  72: /**
  73:  * WHICH KIND OF THING IS BEING READ. Two scopes, because two different rules admit two different
  74:  * viewers: an organisation's non-public data belongs to the organisation's member, and a project's
  75:  * working data belongs to that project's assigned developer.
  76:  */
  77: export type TenantReadScope = 'organization' | 'project';
  78: 
  79: /**
  80:  * THE VIEWER'S STANDING RELATIVE TO THE TARGET â€” never a list, and that is the device rather than a
  81:  * convention.
  82:  *
  83:  * `roleInTargetOrganization` is the role the viewer holds IN THE ORGANISATION BEING READ, and there
  84:  * is nowhere here to put a second role. So an implementation that authorised from the caller's
  85:  * standing in some OTHER organisation cannot express itself through this type at all â€” the same
  86:  * device `orgAdminActionAllowed` uses, and the same reason. `assignedVolunteerOfTargetProject` is the
  87:  * same shape for the project scope: one boolean about THIS project, never a list of projects.
  88:  *
  89:  * EVERY FIELD ARRIVES AS DATA A CALLER READ, so every field is narrowed here rather than trusted.
  90:  */
  91: export type TenantViewer = {
  92:   /** the caller's GLOBAL account type, or `null` when the caller holds no account row at all */
  93:   accountType: AccountType | null;
  94:   /** the caller's role in the organisation being read, or `null` when it holds no membership there */
  95:   roleInTargetOrganization: OrgRole | null;
  96:   /** whether the caller is the assigned developer OF THE PROJECT being read */
  97:   assignedVolunteerOfTargetProject: boolean;
  98: };
  99: 
 100: /**
 101:  * WHY ACCESS WAS GRANTED, carried out of the decision rather than left implicit.
 102:  *
 103:  * It is the same device `OrgAdminRefusalKind` is: a reader that could only see `ok: true` could not
 104:  * tell "the platform administrator reached across accounts" from "this happened to be the caller's
 105:  * own organisation". The basis is what makes the grant's reason readable at all.
 106:  *
 107:  * ITS CONSUMER IS `tests/at/harness/shipped-visibility.selftest.ts`, WHICH DRIVES THIS MODULE
 108:  * DIRECTLY â€” and naming it matters, because this paragraph used to imply the consumer was AT-001.40.
 109:  * IT IS NOT. THE ACCEPTANCE SURFACE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY THE BASIS: `TenantReadOutcome` is a
 110:  * status and a value, or a status and a body, and its own header gives the reason â€” the claim under
 111:  * test is that two whole answers are IDENTICAL, and an outcome carrying more fields would invite an
 112:  * assertion weaker than the criterion. So AT-001.40 makes its reach attributable a different way, and
 113:  * the right way: two different tenants read by ONE administrator, and a NON-ADMIN repeating one of
 114:  * those reads and being refused. Widening a product surface for a test's convenience would be the
 115:  * defect rather than the fix.
 116:  */
 117: export type TenantReadBasis = 'platform-admin' | 'organisation-member' | 'assigned-volunteer';
 118: 
 119: /**
 120:  * A tenant-read decision. Its refusal carries a SENTENCE FOR A READER, and that sentence NEVER
 121:  * travels to the caller â€” `TENANT_NOT_FOUND` is what travels. Keeping the two apart is what stops a
 122:  * helpful refusal becoming the existence oracle the constant exists to remove.
 123:  */
 124: export type TenantReadDecision = { ok: true; basis: TenantReadBasis } | { ok: false; reason: string };
 125: 
 126: /** The account type, or `null` for every value this module does not recognise â€” it fails closed. */
 127: function knownAccountType(raw: unknown): AccountType | null {
 128:   if (typeof raw !== 'string') return null;
 129:   const candidate = raw.trim();
 130:   return (ACCOUNT_TYPES as readonly string[]).includes(candidate) ? (candidate as AccountType) : null;
 131: }
 132: 
 133: /**
 134:  * AT-001.21, .22, .23 and .40 as ONE decision: may this viewer read this thing's non-public data.
 135:  *
 136:  * THE FOUR CLAUSES, each traceable to its criterion:
 137:  *   * A PLATFORM ADMIN reads both scopes, for every account â€” AT-001.40, the founder's d65 ruling
 138:  *     that the admin role spans all accounts.
 139:  *   * AN NGO ACCOUNT reads an organisation's non-public data only where it holds a membership row IN
 140:  *     THAT organisation â€” AT-001.21, whose whole content is that acting in one NGO grants nothing in
 141:  *     another.
 142:  *   * A VOLUNTEER reads a project's working data only where it is THAT project's assigned developer
 143:  *     â€” AT-001.22's denial and AT-001.23's grant, which are the two halves of one rule.
 144:  *   * EVERYBODY ELSE, including a logged-out viewer, reads neither â€” AT-001.24's API half.
 145:  *
 146:  * IT FAILS CLOSED ON EVERY VALUE IT DOES NOT RECOGNISE, the posture `parseOrgRole` states and gives
 147:  * its reasons for: an unknown account type, an unknown role, AN UNKNOWN SCOPE, `undefined`, a number
 148:  * and a missing row all reach the same refusal, and no value widens authority.
 149:  *
 150:  * THE SCOPE IS NAMED OUT LOUD BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE THIS SENTENCE USED TO BE UNTRUE OF. Gate 2
 151:  * measured it: every value other than `'organization'` fell through to the project rule, where an
 152:  * assigned volunteer is allowed, so an unrecognised value WIDENED authority while this paragraph
 153:  * promised the opposite. `tests/at/harness/shipped-visibility.selftest.ts` is the oracle for the
 154:  * whole promise, that branch included.
 155:  *
 156:  * THE PLATFORM-ADMIN BRANCH IS NOW DRIVEN AT TWO LEVELS, and this paragraph used to say it was
 157:  * driven at none. That was true of the slice that landed this module â€” AT-001.21 and AT-001.22 drive
 158:  * no administrator â€” and it stopped being true on 2026-08-13, when the slice that ships the
 159:  * platform-admin policy landed AT-001.40 beside it. The branch is now driven as a UNIT by
 160:  * `tests/at/harness/shipped-visibility.selftest.ts`, and THROUGH A SURFACE by AT-001.40 at both
 161:  * tiers: one administrator reads two organisations' dashboards and two projects' workspaces, where a
 162:  * non-administrator repeating one of those reads is refused. The unit oracle is not the acceptance
 163:  * one, and neither stands in for the other.
 164:  */
 165: export function tenantReadAllowed(viewer: TenantViewer, scope: TenantReadScope): TenantReadDecision {
 166:   const accountType = knownAccountType(viewer?.accountType);
 167:   if (accountType === null) {
 168:     return {
 169:       ok: false,
 170:       reason: 'the caller holds no recognised account type, so it reads no non-public data at all',
 171:     };
 172:   }
 173: 
 174:   // THE ADMINISTRATOR'S REACH IS THE ONE BRANCH THAT DOES NOT READ THE SCOPE. d65: the role spans all
 175:   // accounts, so narrowing it per scope would be building a rule the founder ruled against.
 176:   if (accountType === 'platform_admin') return { ok: true, basis: 'platform-admin' };
 177: 
 178:   if (scope === 'organization') {
 179:     // THE ROLE IS THE ONE IN THE TARGET ORGANISATION, and `parseOrgRole` is what narrows it â€” the
 180:     // same shipped narrowing `update-organization` uses, so an unrecognised value cannot widen
 181:     // anything here either. EITHER role reads: `admin` and `member` are both inside the tenant, and
 182:     // the admin-only axis is a different question that `orgAdminActionAllowed` answers.
 183:     const role = parseOrgRole(viewer?.roleInTargetOrganization);
 184:     if (accountType === 'ngo' && role !== null) return { ok: true, basis: 'organisation-member' };
 185:     return {
 186:       ok: false,
 187:       reason:
 188:         'an organisation\'s non-public data is readable by the accounts seated in THAT organisation only â€” ' +
 189:         'membership is held per organisation, so acting in one organisation grants nothing in another',
 190:     };
 191:   }
 192: 
 193:   if (scope === 'project') {
 194:     if (accountType === 'volunteer' && viewer?.assignedVolunteerOfTargetProject === true) {
 195:       return { ok: true, basis: 'assigned-volunteer' };
 196:     }
 197:     return {
 198:       ok: false,
 199:       reason:
 200:         "a project's working data is readable by that project's assigned developer only â€” " +
 201:         'a volunteer who is not assigned to it reads nothing of it',
 202:     };
 203:   }
 204: 
 205:   // THE SAME FAIL-CLOSED POSTURE `knownAccountType` AND `parseOrgRole` APPLY, on the third argument
 206:   // rather than the first two, and no call site can reach it today because `TenantReadScope` is a
 207:   // union of exactly two strings. Before this branch existed, every OTHER value fell through to the
 208:   // project rule, where an assigned volunteer is allowed â€” so an unrecognised scope WIDENED access
 209:   // while the header above promised the opposite.
 210:   return {
 211:     ok: false,
 212:     reason: 'the read names no recognised scope, so it reads nothing at all',
 213:   };
 214: }
 215: 
 216: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ what the world may see */
 217: 
 218: /**
 219:  * WHAT A PROJECT SHOWS THE WORLD â€” one statement of what "public" is, so the word names a value this
 220:  * repository can point at rather than a habit each surface repeats.
 221:  *
 222:  * THREE FIELDS, AND THE ABSENCES ARE THE SUBSTANCE. `organizationId` and `assignedVolunteerId` are
 223:  * both fields the WORKSPACE projection carries and this one deliberately does not: the first is an
 224:  * internal identifier a stranger has no use for, and the second names a person. AT-001.22's body
 225:  * asserts each absence by name rather than counting fields, because a count passes while the wrong
 226:  * field is present.
 227:  */
 228: export type PublicProjectView = {
 229:   projectId: string;
 230:   projectName: string;
 231:   organizationName: string;
 232: };
 233: 
 234: /**
 235:  * The public projection of one project â€” the whole of what `public-project-page` may answer.
 236:  *
 237:  * IT TAKES THE TWO ROWS RATHER THAN A JOINED SHAPE, so the caller cannot pass a workspace projection
 238:  * in and have extra fields survive: this function builds the answer field by field and copies
 239:  * nothing wholesale.
 240:  */
 241: export function publicProjectView(
 242:   project: { id: string; name: string },
 243:   organization: { name: string },
 244: ): PublicProjectView {
 245:   return {
 246:     projectId: project.id,
 247:     projectName: project.name,
 248:     organizationName: organization.name,
 249:   };
 250: }
--- supabase/functions/organization-dashboard/index.ts ---
   1: /**
   2:  * `organization-dashboard` â€” AT-001.21's "NGO A's non-public data (â€¦ dashboard)", as a surface.
   3:  *
   4:  * IT IS NOT A GENERAL READER. It answers ONE projection about ONE organisation, the way
   5:  * `update-organization` refuses to be a general organisation-profile editor. A general reader would
   6:  * satisfy the criterion today and become the leak the criterion is about the moment a column is added
   7:  * to a table it reads.
   8:  *
   9:  * THE DECISION IS THE SHARED MODULE'S â€” `tenantReadAllowed` in `../_shared/visibility.ts`, the same
  10:  * function the acceptance suite drives at both tiers. That is what keeps a loop-tier green a claim
  11:  * about shipped code rather than about a copy.
  12:  *
  13:  * ============================================================================================
  14:  * THE READ ORDER IS THE NO-EXISTENCE-ORACLE PROPERTY. DO NOT MOVE THE TARGET READ.
  15:  * ============================================================================================
  16:  *
  17:  * Four reads, and the organisation row â€” the TARGET â€” is the LAST of them. Every read a decision or a
  18:  * projection needs is issued BEFORE it, and NOTHING is read after it.
  19:  *
  20:  *   1. the caller's account row              â€” keyed on the CALLER
  21:  *   2. the target organisation's seat rows   â€” keyed on the organisation id, on a DIFFERENT table
  22:  *   3. the target organisation's projects    â€” keyed on the organisation id, on a DIFFERENT table
  23:  *   4. THE ORGANISATION ROW ITSELF           â€” the target, last
  24:  *
  25:  * WHY IT MATTERS. If a lookup came AFTER the target read, a fault in it would be reachable only on
  26:  * the path where the target exists: a real foreign identifier would answer 502 while an identifier
  27:  * that names nothing had already answered 404, and those two answers are an existence oracle sitting
  28:  * outside `TENANT_NOT_FOUND`. With nothing after the target read, a fault answers the same 502 either
  29:  * way, by construction rather than by care.
  30:  *
  31:  * IT DOES COST A READ THE ANSWER MAY NOT USE â€” the projects of an organisation this caller will be
  32:  * refused. That is not waste, it IS the property: the work the surface does is the same whatever the
  33:  * answer turns out to be.
  34:  */
  35: 
  36: import type { AccountType } from '../_shared/accounts.ts';
  37: import {
  38:   edgeHandler,
  39:   json,
  40:   readJsonBody,
  41:   readRows,
  42:   refusal,
  43:   requireEnv,
  44:   resolveCaller,
  45: } from '../_shared/edge.ts';
  46: import { parseOrgRole } from '../_shared/memberships.ts';
  47: import { TENANT_NOT_FOUND, tenantReadAllowed } from '../_shared/visibility.ts';
  48: 
  49: const SUPABASE_URL = requireEnv('SUPABASE_URL');
  50: const ANON_KEY = requireEnv('SUPABASE_ANON_KEY', 'SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY');
  51: const SERVICE_ROLE_KEY = requireEnv('SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY', 'SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY');
  52: 
  53: /**
  54:  * WHAT AN OUTAGE ANSWERS, and it names no identifier.
  55:  *
  56:  * The sentence is the SAME for every target, which is the half of the property this constant carries:
  57:  * two faulted reads must be indistinguishable, and a reason that quoted the organisation id would
  58:  * make them distinguishable while looking helpful.
  59:  */
  60: function readFailed(): Response {
  61:   return json(
  62:     { ok: false, reason: 'the organisation dashboard could not read what it needs, so no decision was made' },
  63:     502,
  64:   );
  65: }
  66: 
  67: Deno.serve(edgeHandler('organization-dashboard', async (request: Request): Promise<Response> => {
  68:   if (request.method !== 'POST') return refusal('organization-dashboard accepts POST only', 405);
  69: 
  70:   const caller = await resolveCaller(request, SUPABASE_URL, ANON_KEY);
